In production atomic are used on the flags, counter ,enum, reference count on shared ptr is usingh the atomic internally 

First: what “atomic” actually guarantees (important)

When you write:

std::atomic<int> a{0};
std::atomic<int> b{0};


Atomic guarantees ONLY this:

Each individual read/write of a is safe

Each individual read/write of b is safe

❗ Atomic does NOT guarantee:

both change together

both are seen at the same time

ordering between a++ and b++

Now let’s replay the exact execution step-by-step

Initial state:

a = 0
b = 0


Code in both threads:

a++;
b++;

Timeline (this is the key)
Step 1

Thread 1

a++;   // a = 1


State:

a = 1
b = 0

Step 2

Thread 2

a++;   // a = 2


State:

a = 2
b = 0


👉 At this moment, this is a perfectly valid, observable state

Why?

a was updated atomically

b has not been updated yet

No rule says they must move together

Step 3

Thread 2

b++;   // b = 1


State:

a = 2
b = 1

Step 4

Thread 1

b++;   // b = 2


Final state:

a = 2
b = 2

🔥 THE CRITICAL MOMENT 🔥

Between Step 2 and Step 4, the program is in this state:

a = 2
b = 1

Why is this allowed?

Because:

a++ and b++ are separate operations

Atomic ≠ grouped

Other threads are allowed to observe memory between operations

“But b is also atomic — why isn’t it 2?”

Because b++ hasn’t happened yet in Thread 1.

Atomic does NOT mean:

“all atomics move together”

It means:

“each one moves safely when it moves”

This is the key misunderstanding (read carefully)

❌ Wrong mental model:

“If two atomics are updated in one function, other threads see them together”

✅ Correct mental model:

“Other threads can observe memory between any two atomic operations”

Why mutex fixes this immediately
Mutex version:
lock(m);
a++;
b++;
unlock(m);


Now:

No thread can observe memory inside the block

a and b change as a unit

Intermediate state is invisible

Ultra-simple analogy (this usually clicks)
Atomic = updating numbers on a whiteboard

Anyone can look between strokes

Mutex = closing the door

Nobody sees until you’re done

Final rule (now fully justified)

Atomic guarantees correctness of a variable.
Mutex guarantees correctness of a situation.

Interview-grade one-liner

If interviewer asks:

“Why can atomics break logic even though they’re safe?”

Answer:

“Atomics make individual updates safe, but other threads can observe intermediate states between atomic operations.”

That answer is spot-on.

If you want, next I can:

show how CAS tries to fix this

explain why lock-free code is hard

give a quick quiz to test this understanding

